% Expressions SEL itself rejects, which the translator used to translate.
%
% For four milestones the translator answered "can this be pushed into that
% database" without ever asking "is this a SEL expression". LEFT("abc", -1) is
% not one -- SEL raises E_RANGE -- and it translated cleanly into all four
% dialects, where MariaDB answered '', PostgreSQL answered 'ab' and SQLite
% answered 'abc'. Three servers, three answers, none of them SEL's, reported as
% a successful translation. That is the core promise inverted: a translation
% that passes is supposed to mean the contract holds.
%
% Where every argument is written down, the answer is knowable here, so the
% translator asks SEL's own evaluator and refuses what SEL refuses. The check is
% validation and not constant folding: the value is computed and discarded, and
% the SQL emitted for an expression that passes is byte-identical to what was
% emitted before. Folding would have blinded the oracle, which exists to compare
% the database's version of an operation against SEL's.
%
% Positions are asserted because they are the point: the position reported is
% SEL's own innermost failing node, not the call this check was entered at.
% `LEFT("abc", 1 - 2)` blames the `-`.

### name: const.range.left-negative-length
--- note
The row this whole file exists for. Was `LEFT('abc', -1)` on every dialect.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
LEFT("abc", -1)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:13
===
### name: const.range.left-negative-length-postgresql
--- note
Same expression, different wrong answer: PostgreSQL's left(str, -1) drops the
last character and returns 'ab'. A refusal shared by all four dialects is the
only outcome that is SEL's.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
LEFT("abc", -1)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:13
===
### name: const.range.right-negative-length
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
RIGHT("abc", -1)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:14
===
### name: const.range.substr-zero-position
--- note
SEL's string positions are 1-based, so 0 is out of range. SQLite and PostgreSQL
answer 'abc' and MariaDB answers ''.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
SUBSTR("abc", 0)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:15
===
### name: const.range.substr-negative-position
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
SUBSTR("abc", -1)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:15
===
### name: const.range.substr-negative-length
--- note
The one row where a server did fail rather than answer: PostgreSQL raises
"negative substring length not allowed". Two of the four still answered.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
SUBSTR("abc", 1, -1)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:18
===
### name: const.range.padl-negative-width
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
PADL("x", -1, "0")
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:11
===
### name: const.range.repeat-negative-count
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
REPEAT("x", -1)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:13
===
### name: const.range.find-zero-start
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
FIND("a", "banana", 0)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:21
===
### name: const.range.round-negative-places
--- note
MariaDB rounds to tens and answers 0; SQLite ignores the sign and answers 2.
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
ROUND(1.5, -1)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:12
===
### name: const.range.char-negative-code-point
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
CHAR(-1)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:6
===
### name: const.range.code-of-empty-string
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
CODE("")
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:6
===
### name: const.range.power-negative-exponent
--- note
Every dialect answers 0.01, and SEL raises: POWER's exponent is a whole number
because the result has to be exact.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
POWER(10, -2)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:11
===
### name: const.notint.left-fractional-length
--- note
MariaDB rounds 2.7 up to 3 and answers 'abc'; SQLite truncates to 2 and answers
'ab'. Both are answers to a question SEL declines to ask.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
LEFT("abc", 2.7)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:13
===
### name: const.notint.substr-fractional-position
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
SUBSTR("abc", 1.5)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:15
===
### name: const.notint.repeat-fractional-count
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
REPEAT("x", 2.5)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:13
===
### name: const.notint.round-fractional-places
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
ROUND(1.234, 1.5)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:14
===
### name: const.notint.padl-fractional-width
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
PADL("x", 3.5, "0")
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:11
===
### name: const.notnum.left-length-with-leading-space
--- note
Numbers are text in SEL (spec §4) and " 2" is not one of them. Every dialect
trims and answers 'ab' -- input laxity that produces a value where SEL produces
an error, which no caveat can express.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
LEFT("abc", " 2")
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:13
===
### name: const.badarg.padl-empty-fill
--- note
An empty pad string cannot reach any width. MariaDB answers '' and PostgreSQL
answers 'x'.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
PADL("x", 3, "")
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:14
===
### name: const.divzero.divide
--- note
MariaDB and SQLite answer NULL, PostgreSQL raises. This is the divergence
sqlite.json's `not-caveatable` note and §11.2 record, and where both operands
are written down it stops being a divergence at all.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
1 / 0
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:3
===
### name: const.divzero.modulo
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
5 % 0
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:3
===
### name: const.divzero.decimal-divisor
--- note
0.0 is zero, and being written with a scale does not make it a different
divisor.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
2.50 % 0.0
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:6
===
### name: const.computed.argument-is-folded-first
--- note
`1 - 2` is a constant subtree, so the argument it computes is knowable and the
call is checked. The position blames the `-`, which is SEL's own innermost
failing node rather than the call this check was entered at.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
LEFT("abc", 1 - 2)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:15
===
### name: const.aggregate.body-over-a-literal-list
--- note
An aggregate over a literal list is constant, binder and all, so the body is
checked too -- once, at the aggregate, rather than once per unrolled element.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
ALL((1, 2), LEFT("ab", -1) $== _)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:24
===
### name: const.valid.still-translates
--- note
The other half of the promise. An expression SEL accepts is emitted exactly as
it was before this check existed; nothing is folded away.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
LEFT("abc", 1 + 1)
--- expect
LEFT('abc', (1 + 1))
===
### name: const.valid.zero-length-is-not-out-of-range
--- note
0 is a legal length and -1 is not, and the check has to keep them apart.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
LEFT("abc", 0)
--- expect
LEFT('abc', 0)
===
### name: const.column.cannot-be-checked
--- note
The residual risk, pinned so it is a decision rather than an oversight. N is a
column: its value is not known here, so nothing validates it and the database
answers whatever it answers. Argument validation happens where SEL's does, at
the value. See docs/internals/sql-translation.md §11.4.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"N": {"kind": "column", "column": "n", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
LEFT("abc", N)
--- expect
LEFT('abc', `n`)
===
### name: const.column.negation-of-a-column-is-not-constant
--- note
`-N` looks like the literal `-1` and is not one. The constancy test is about
leaves, not about shape.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"N": {"kind": "column", "column": "n", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
LEFT("abc", -N)
--- expect
LEFT('abc', (-`n`))
===
### name: const.abort.keeps-its-own-refusal
--- note
ABORT("x") is constant and raises, and reporting it as an expression SEL
rejects would be true and useless. Refusals the translator already had are
reached first, because the check runs after the node translates rather than
before.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
ABORT("x")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:1
===
### name: const.regex.keeps-its-own-refusal
--- note
Same ordering, second witness: an unportable pattern is refused by name on the
way past and never reaches the constant check.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
RMATCH(IF(TRUE, '\p{L}', 'x'), "x")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:8
===
### name: const.lazy.dead-branch-is-still-checked
--- note
SEL is lazy, so this whole expression is constant and evaluates to FALSE
without ever dividing. Checking only the outermost constant node would accept
it and put `(1 / 0)` in the SQL, while the same division beside a column was
refused -- opposite answers for one expression, decided by whether the operand
next to it happened to be written down. So every constant compound node is
checked, not just the outermost. MariaDB and PostgreSQL both short-circuit and
would not have raised; §11.2 records that SQL does not promise that.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
FALSE AND (1 / 0 > 0)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:14
===
### name: const.lazy.unselected-if-arm-is-still-checked
--- note
The same rule through IF rather than AND: SEL never evaluates the ELSE arm.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
IF(TRUE, "ok", LEFT("abc", -1))
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:28
===
### name: const.lazy.unselected-arm-beside-a-column
--- note
And the case that made the asymmetry visible: with a column as the condition
the IF is not constant, so the walk reaches the arm on its own. Both spellings
now refuse, which is the point.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"F": {"kind": "column", "column": "f", "type": "BOOL"}}
--- source
IF(F, "ok", LEFT("abc", -1))
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:25
===
### name: const.scale.integer-valued-decimal-is-an-integer
--- note
2.0 is a whole number written with a scale, and SEL accepts it where it rejects
2.7. All four servers agree, so this is a case rather than a refusal -- pinned
because the check has to keep `2.0` and `2.7` apart, and the obvious way to
write it would not.
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
LEFT("abc", 2.0)
--- expect
left(CAST('abc' AS TEXT), CAST(2.0 AS INTEGER))
===
### name: const.binding.value-is-a-constant-the-translator-has
--- note
§5.4 calls a value binding "a constant supplied at translation time, inlined as
a literal", and Translator::variable hands it straight to literal(). So this is
exactly as knowable as LEFT("abc", -1) -- and before the check learned about
bindings it was exactly as wrong: SEL raised E_RANGE and the four servers
answered '', '', 'ab' and ''. §11.4's headline defect, still open through the
documented way a host passes a parameter.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"X": {"kind": "value", "value": "-1", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
LEFT("abc", X)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID
===
### name: const.binding.value-zero-divisor
--- dialect
postgresql
--- bindings
{"Z": {"kind": "value", "value": "0", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
1 / Z
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID
===
### name: const.binding.value-that-is-not-a-number
--- note
MariaDB and MySQL answer 1.0, SQLite 1, PostgreSQL raises. SEL says E_NOT_NUM.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"S": {"kind": "value", "value": "abc"}}
--- source
S + 1
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID
===
### name: const.binding.a-column-is-still-not-constant
--- note
The line between the two: a value binding is a value the translator has, a
column is not. This is the residual risk of §11.4 and it stays.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"N": {"kind": "column", "column": "n", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
1 / N
--- expect
(1 / `n`)
===
### name: const.assign.value-is-validated-where-it-is-recorded
--- note
Stage 1 drops a definition nothing reads, so this translated to `TRUE` and every
server answered TRUE where SEL raises at the assignment. Validated in
Normalise::record, before substitute, because after that the subtree is gone.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
A = 1 / 0; TRUE
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID
===
### name: const.assign.unread-definition-is-still-checked
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
A = LEFT("abc", -1); TRUE
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID
===
### name: const.assign.indexed-value-is-checked
--- note
An indexed assignment builds a clist, which the constant test refuses to walk
and COUNT never renders -- so the division was invisible twice over and the
answer was 1.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
R[1] = 1 / 0; COUNT(R)
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID
===
### name: const.assign.a-valid-assignment-still-inlines
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
A = 2 + 3; A * 2
--- expect
((2 + 3) * 2)
===
### name: const.numeric.text-literal-beside-a-column
--- note
The rest of this file is closed expressions, where the whole node is knowable.
This half is what the check missed for as long as it existed: a constant sitting
in a numeric position *beside* something that is not constant.

`"x"` is not a number, so SEL raises E_NOT_NUM here whatever `T` holds — the
answer does not depend on the column at all. It translated anyway, and
CAST('x' AS DECIMAL) is 0 on MariaDB, MySQL and SQLite, so the predicate became
`0 = 0` and matched every row; PostgreSQL raised 22P02 at plan time. Reported
from the field against a MariaDB EAV schema.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
T == "x"
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:6
===
### name: const.numeric.text-literal-in-arithmetic
--- note
The same for arithmetic, where MariaDB emitted `t + 'x'` with no cast at all.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
T + "x"
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:5
===
### name: const.numeric.reassociation-does-not-hide-it
--- note
The case that says the check is per node rather than per expression. The old
rule asked "is this whole node constant", so `T + (1 + "x")` refused and
`(T + 1) + "x"` — the same expression, differently parenthesised — translated.
Whether a defect is caught may not depend on where the author put brackets.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
(T + 1) + "x"
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:11
===
### name: const.numeric.constant-subtree-beside-a-column
--- note
Not just a literal: any subtree with no non-constant leaf is knowable, and its
value is what has to be a number. The position blames the `&`, which is the
innermost node SEL itself blames.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
T == ("a" & "b")
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:11
===
### name: const.numeric.value-binding-beside-a-column
--- note
A `value` binding is a constant the translator has — spec §5.4 calls it "a
constant supplied at translation time" — so it is knowable for exactly the same
reason a literal is. `S + 1` already refused because that node is wholly
constant; adding a column beside it used to be enough to turn the check off.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "NUM"}, "S": {"kind": "value", "value": "abc"}}
--- source
T + S
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:5
===
### name: const.numeric.unary-minus
--- note
Unary `-` reads its operand as a number too. This one already refused, because
`-"x"` is itself a wholly-constant node and the old rule caught it there; it is
pinned so that the per-operand check cannot regress what the per-node check
already covered.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
-"x" + T
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:2
===
### name: const.numeric.a-text-column-still-compares-numerically
--- note
The other half, and the reason this check is about the constant's *value* and
never about a declared kind. SEL's `==` compares numerically, so "5.00" == "5"
is TRUE and a bare `=` between two text columns would say FALSE. A TEXT column
holding numerals is a legitimate schema and must keep translating; refusing on
the declared kind would break it. Pinned as op.compare.coerce-variant-when-a-
side-is-not, and pinned again here so this check cannot grow into that one.
The same program as op.compare.coerce-variant-when-a-side-is-not in
02-operators.sqlt, deliberately: there it pins the operator rule, here that the
constant folder leaves it alone.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"A": {"kind": "column", "column": "a", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
A == 5
--- expect
(CASE WHEN (`a` REGEXP '\\A-?[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?\\z') THEN CAST(`a` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) ELSE NULL END = 5)
===
### name: const.numeric.a-numeric-text-literal-is-fine
--- note
And a constant that *is* a number is not the target: SEL evaluates this, so the
translator must too.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
T == "5"
--- expect
(CAST(`t` AS DECIMAL(65,10)) = CAST('5' AS DECIMAL(65,10)))
===
### name: const.residual.argument-constraint-beside-a-column
--- note
A KNOWN DIVERGENCE, pinned so it is recorded rather than forgotten.

SEL raises E_RANGE here for every value of T -- the offending argument is the
-1, which is written down, and only LEFT knows its length may not be negative.
So this is not the unknowable residual that const.column.cannot-be-checked
pins; it is knowable, and it still translates.

The const.numeric.* family closed the operand half of this: a constant in a
numeric position is checked wherever it sits. It cannot close this half,
because -1 IS a number and passes that test. Closing it needs SEL's own
per-builtin argument validation reached from the translator, which is a
different mechanism and not yet built.

When it is built, this case will fail. Change it to an error then, deliberately.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}
--- source
LEFT(T, -1)
--- expect
LEFT(`t`, (-1))
===

% Found by the SQL fuzz lane (seed 20260905): a constant TEXT that is a number, in an arithmetic position (+ - * / %, unary minus, MIN and MAX arguments), is spelled as the exact numeric literal it stands for -- MariaDB and MySQL would read the quoted string as a DOUBLE ('0.1' + '0.2' = 0.3 is false there); the executing evidence is sql/oracle/expressions.selo. SQLite keeps the text and the dialect's numeric cast handles it.

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.add.mariadb
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
"0.1" + "0.2" == 0.3
--- expect
((0.1 + 0.2) = 0.3)
===

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.max.mariadb
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
MAX("10", "9") == 10
--- expect
(GREATEST(10, 9) = 10)
===

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.folded-call.mariadb
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
LTRIM("41") + 1
--- expect
(41 + 1)
===

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.unary.mariadb
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
-"5" + 1
--- expect
((-5) + 1)
===

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.scale-kept.mariadb
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
"1.10" + 2 > 3
--- expect
((1.10 + 2) > 3)
===

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.add.postgresql
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
"0.1" + "0.2" == 0.3
--- expect
((CAST(0.1 AS NUMERIC) + CAST(0.2 AS NUMERIC)) = 0.3)
===

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.max.postgresql
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
MAX("10", "9") == 10
--- expect
(greatest(10, 9) = 10)
===

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.folded-call.postgresql
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
LTRIM("41") + 1
--- expect
(CAST(41 AS NUMERIC) + CAST(1 AS NUMERIC))
===

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.unary.postgresql
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
-"5" + 1
--- expect
(CAST((-CAST(5 AS NUMERIC)) AS NUMERIC) + CAST(1 AS NUMERIC))
===

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.scale-kept.postgresql
--- dialect
postgresql
--- source
"1.10" + 2 > 3
--- expect
((CAST(1.10 AS NUMERIC) + CAST(2 AS NUMERIC)) > 3)
===

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.add.sqlite
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
"0.1" + "0.2" == 0.3
--- expect
(CAST(('0.1' + '0.2') AS NUMERIC) = CAST('0.3' AS NUMERIC))
===

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.max.sqlite
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
MAX("10", "9") == 10
--- expect
(CAST(max(CAST('10' AS NUMERIC), CAST('9' AS NUMERIC)) AS NUMERIC) = CAST('10' AS NUMERIC))
===

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.folded-call.sqlite
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
LTRIM("41") + 1
--- expect
('41' + '1')
===

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.unary.sqlite
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
-"5" + 1
--- expect
((-'5') + '1')
===

### name: const.arith.numeric-text-is-the-number.scale-kept.sqlite
--- dialect
sqlite
--- source
"1.10" + 2 > 3
--- expect
(CAST(('1.10' + '2') AS NUMERIC) > CAST('3' AS NUMERIC))
===


### name: const.binding-form.link-right-source-is-read-where-the-call-stands
--- note
Whether a binding call is constant is read off the manifest's form for its argument count
(spec/builtins.json), as stage 1 and the dependency walk read it. LINK's second argument is
its right SOURCE, not a binder: ORDERS is a relation, so the definition is not constant, is
not validated, and -- read by nothing -- is dropped. A host that took the generic shape
(three arguments: the second is the binder) asked SEL to evaluate it without ORDERS and
refused with E_SQL_INVALID.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
X = COUNT(LINK(LIST(1), ORDERS, TRUE)); TRUE
--- expect
TRUE
===

### name: const.binding-form.a-three-argument-top-by-has-no-binder
--- note
TOP_BY's three-argument form is (source, key, count): ORDERS is the KEY, read per element, and
names a relation, so nothing here is constant.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ORDERS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "orders", "alias": "o", "fields": {"ID": {"table": "o", "column": "id", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
X = TOP_BY(LIST(1), ORDERS, 1); TRUE
--- expect
TRUE
===

### name: const.binding-form.the-body-sees-the-key
--- note
The form binds _K as well as _ in the body, so the definition is constant and SEL's verdict on
it, E_DIV_ZERO at the division, is the translation's.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
X = MAP(LIST(1, 2), _K / 0); TRUE
--- error
E_SQL_INVALID 1:24
===
